(t)=7x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229143 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d611c75-818f-508f-a7a4-49edb32baa41" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :quest