l value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 6{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229144 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e58bb6-6d90-5171-bba2-6ffa5a0c91d9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential: Shifted Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfee9ed8-1867-53c1-a47d-d52b275699cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e^{4(t-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfee9ed8-1867-53c1-a47d-d52b275699cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e^{4(t-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445588} :answer-field/id #uuid "c45903a1-fe5a-5b4c-96af-6a0c00ecf707" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445604} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f456005-43ef-5ff5-9026-4d0917c6ca81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f456005-43ef-5ff5-9026-4d0917c6ca81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445568} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa590e88-0053-5685-8a5c-24106590dec4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e674cbe1-254a-5d93-a9b4-be22a2029aaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-335427" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(3) \\\\ y(3)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-3)}\\,x(3) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-3)} & (t-3){e}^{4(t-3)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-3)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,-{e}^{4(t-3)}\\, \\\\ \\,0\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229144 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e58bb6-6d90-5171-bba2-6ffa5a0c91d9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential: Shifted Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac977920-e0a4-5694-ad1f-49081b7d03d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{t-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310141} {:answer/id #uuid "0344a228-63e9-598d-8026-7a699136dec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e^{t-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 1